Yes — **the data garden is the product**. The vibe-coding robot app is just one downstream customer.

The right framing is:

> **POW becomes the canonical machine-readable graph of real-world buildability.**

A robotics startup should be able to ask POW:

> “What is the cheapest, available, manufacturable way to implement this function right now?”

and get an answer grounded in actual Chinese parts, current stock, package availability, alternates, fabrication constraints, historical pricing, and supplier reliability.

There are competitors, but I don’t see a free system that gives the full thing you’re describing.

Octopart/Nexar are strong at global electronic-component search, distributor pricing and availability. LCSC/EasyEDA are already extremely useful for Chinese parts and design assets; EasyEDA advertises 600k+ directly searchable components, and current APIs/community tooling expose part specs, stock, pricing, symbols, footprints and datasheets. JLCPCB now has official component, PCB and 3D-printing APIs too. ([JLCPCB][1])

But those systems mostly answer:

> **“Find me part X.”**

POW should answer:

> **“I need function X. What are every viable ways to instantiate it, what do they cost, and what downstream machine designs do they enable?”**

That distinction is enormous.

