## I think this slightly changes the mission of `powparts`

Not:

> **Collect parts.**

Instead:

> **Observe the changing cost, availability, composition and substitutability of physical capabilities.**

That is a much tighter mandate.

`powparts` can know millions of identities, but it should **cultivate history only where time creates value**.

And if I had to choose the first five gardens inside it, I'd start with:

1. **Reference BOM cost indexes** — robots, edge compute, batteries, solar, EVSE.
2. **Frontier component time series** — actuators, sensors, compute, batteries, power electronics.
3. **Open-hardware BOM history** — especially robotics/energy.
4. **Manufacturing benchmark quotes** — PCB/PCBA/3DP/CNC.
5. **Observed composition + substitution graph** — what actually works with what.

Those five give us much more defensible information than downloading eight million catalogue rows.

The rule for the entire POW project could almost be:

> **Don't store what can be queried later. Store what only exists because we were watching.**







Yes. The stronger starting point is **machine-first, then explode downward into parts**.

Take a real robot or open-source build, freeze that exact version, decompose it, then follow every component sideways into replacements, substitutes, newer revisions, cheaper Chinese equivalents, failures, and observed alternatives. That gives `powparts` a reason to exist from day one instead of becoming a giant undifferentiated catalogue. ([UnitreeRobotics][1])

The graph becomes:

```text
ROBOT / DEVICE
   ↓ contains
SUBSYSTEM
   ↓ contains
PART
   ↓
├─ cheaper substitute
├─ drop-in substitute
├─ Chinese equivalent
├─ higher-performance substitute
├─ repair replacement
└─ newer revision
```

Then you repeat that with GitHub hardware designs, commercial teardowns, homelab boxes, speakers, lights, EV chargers, inverters, miners, cameras, sensors. Eventually you learn **the design grammar of physical machines** from observed reality rather than trying to invent an ontology upfront.

That has two very different outputs. One is obvious: future robot-design systems query POW because they need grounded components and compatibility. The second is potentially more interesting commercially: POW notices that a component or subsystem has become unusually cheap relative to what it enables and identifies **new consumer products you can now economically construct**.

For example:

```text
2026:
depth camera       £65
edge compute       £90
2-axis mechanism   £45
motor control      £22
printed housing    £28

total              £250
```

Two years later:

```text
depth camera       £18
edge compute       £32
2-axis mechanism   £19
motor control      £7
printed housing    £9

total              £85
```

Now suddenly a £149 autonomous camera product, desktop robot, tracking speaker, inspection device or weird home object becomes viable. **POW should detect that transition.**

That is what I mean by learning the physical capability frontier.

## There already are Chinese companies doing surprisingly close pieces of the final assembly problem

There is not yet one obvious “prompt → arbitrary finished robot delivered” commodity service that solves everything. But China already has nearly every intermediate primitive.

A particularly relevant example is **Fdata**. It explicitly offers custom robot OEM/ODM: give them a product definition and target price, and they say they can handle industrial design, electronics, mechanics, functional prototypes, verification, mass production and delivery. They advertise 20 assembly lines and 11 SMT lines. ([Fdata Robot][2])

That is remarkably close to the eventual endpoint:

```text
requirements
→ engineering
→ prototype
→ validation
→ manufacture
→ delivery
```

What's missing is a generalized machine intelligence layer **before** that factory interaction.

Another Shenzhen company I found, **Jiejia / one-stop-mfg**, is almost comically aligned with your thesis at the PCB/system level. It advertises one-stop design and manufacturing specifically for robot wheel-leg drive boards, robot power boards, dexterous-hand PCBAs and linear-joint controllers. Its service includes design, component procurement, DFX review, PCB prototyping, SMT/THT, testing, certification support, aging/coating and failure analysis. ([One Stop MFG][3])

So someone can already say:

> “Here is the controller we need.”

and have a Shenzhen company turn that into working assembled electronics.

There are also firms doing **box-build/system integration**, where they don't stop at the PCB. POE, for example, advertises PCB fabrication, sourcing, PCBA, testing, enclosure-level assembly and final packaging, including work in robotics and IoT. 

On the mechanical side, Shenzhen contract manufacturers already accept STEP files and produce one-offs or low-volume parts via CNC, 3D printing, sheet metal, casting and injection molding. E-Make, for example, advertises 1–100 unit production, 2–7 day prototype lead times and one-stop machining/casting/sheet-metal/molding. ([E-make][4])

So the current ecosystem is approximately:

```text
POW / DESIGN AGENT
        ↓

electronics design ──→ PCBA house
mechanical CAD ──────→ CNC / 3DP house
commodity parts ─────→ Shenzhen suppliers
robot platform ──────→ ODM robot factory

        ↓

system assembly
        ↓
test
        ↓
ship
```

You don't need a magical factory. **The factories already exist. The routing layer is missing.**

## Unitree itself is already edging toward customization

Unitree now explicitly advertises custom robot services, including industrial design for legged robots, motion-program customization, algorithm optimization, remote diagnostics and “integrated hardware-software co-design consultation.” ([UnitreeRobotics][1])

And their newer R1-D is intentionally presented as a development platform with open programming interfaces, RGB/depth interfaces and end-effector interfaces that can take different actuators. ([UnitreeRobotics][5])

That is another signal of where things are moving:

> **robot as fixed product**
>
> becomes
>
> **robot as configurable platform**

POW sits underneath the next step:

> **machine assembled from configurable primitives.**

