## Assembly is the problem — but also another dataset

Don't solve it abstractly.

**Observe how existing machines are assembled.**

For each seed robot, add:

```text
ASSEMBLY

part A
   fastened_to part B
   4 × M4×12

part B
   connected_to controller C
   JST-XH-4

motor
   coupled_to gearbox
   keyed 8mm shaft

PCB
   mounted_to chassis
   4 × M3 standoff
```

Suddenly the graph isn't merely `contains`.

It's:

```text
contains
mounted_to
bolted_to
wired_to
soldered_to
coupled_to
powered_by
communicates_with
```

That is **assembly knowledge**.

An AI can learn a huge amount merely from enough actual designs.

CAD gives geometry.

BOM gives identity.

Firmware gives behavior.

Assembly edges connect them.

That combination is powerful.

## And custom hardware doesn't need to start with full custom engineering

There are three increasingly difficult levels:

```text
LEVEL 1 — COMPOSE
off-the-shelf modules

LEVEL 2 — ADAPT
custom enclosure / brackets / wiring / small PCB

LEVEL 3 — INTEGRATE
custom PCBA + custom mechanical design
```

The future vibe-coded hardware system should **prefer Level 1 whenever possible**.

Example:

> tracking camera

could initially be:

```text
N100 / Pi
USB camera
commodity pan/tilt module
USB-C PSU
3D printed enclosure
```

No new PCB.

Once 500 people build it, POW discovers:

> These five modules recur constantly.

Then it collapses them into:

```text
custom board
custom enclosure
```

That's how hardware startups already evolve, except POW could automate the detection.

